{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour before the rain actually begins to fall. The air turns metallic and cool, and the streetlights flicker on too early, as if they also sense something shifting above the rooftops. On those evenings I walk without a destination, letting my shoes find the familiar cracks in the sidewalk while my mind wanders somewhere less practical. I pass the bakery that always smells of cinnamon even after closing, the bookstore with its crooked sign, and the small park where an old man feeds pigeons from a paper bag. Everyone I meet seems slightly more honest in that waiting light. Strangers nod, neighbors linger on porches, and children race the darkening clouds home on bicycles that squeak with every determined push of the pedals. I think we are all briefly aware, then, that ordinary days are not guaranteed to stay ordinary. A phone call can rearrange a life, a letter can reopen a door, and a storm can rewrite the map of a coastline overnight. So I try to pay attention before anything dramatic forces me to. I notice how my mother laughs with her whole shoulders, how my friend pauses before answering serious questions, and how the neighbor's cat always chooses the warmest step. These details feel like small anchors. When the first drops finally arrive, they darken the pavement in uneven circles and release that clean, earthy smell that no candle has ever copied correctly. I head home then, unhurried, grateful for almost everything.",
